{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Jelly Falls: Bacterial Feast on a Sinking Salp Bloom",
    "date": "2026-08-09",
    "lesson": "When a bloom of gelatinous zooplankton (salps, jellyfish) dies en masse, their carcasses sink rapidly as 'jelly falls' - carbon-rich, low-nitrogen marine snow that copiotrophic bacteria colonize within hours. Because jelly tissue is mostly mucopolysaccharide and lipid membrane with comparatively little protein, colonizers that lead with lipases and carbohydrate-active enzymes outcompete protein specialists. Enzymatic hydrolysis is fast but the falls are patchy and ephemeral, so chemotaxis toward a fall is as important as digesting it once found. This mirrors real sinking-particle studies showing rapid, taxon-specific succession on gelatinous versus fecal-pellet marine snow.",
    "citation": "Lebrato et al. 2012, Limnol. Oceanogr.; Titelman et al. 2006 jellyfish carcass decomposition studies",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Jelly-fall carbon flux and copiotroph succession on gelatinous marine snow, temperate shelf seas"
  },
